<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
Storing user passwords in a database in clear text means that anyone who obtains a copy of the
database, through a backup, a SQL or NoSQL injection, or a misconfigured replica, learns every
user's password. Because passwords are commonly reused, this also enables credential-stuffing
attacks against other services.
</p>
<p>
This query tracks values whose names indicate a password (for example <code>password</code>,
<code>passwd</code> or a <code>Password</code> struct field) to documents and statement parameters written
by MongoDB (<code>gopkg.in/mgo</code>, <code>go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver</code>), GORM, and
<code>database/sql</code> <code>Exec</code> calls that insert or update data.
It also recognizes unnamed string or byte-slice values bound to password-named columns in
simple constant SQL <code>INSERT INTO table (columns) VALUES (placeholders)</code> statements.
</p>
<p>
<b>Known limitations:</b> Constant SQL statements beginning with a common table expression
(<code>WITH ... INSERT</code>, for example) are not recognized as database writes.
For simple constant <code>UPDATE</code> statements, only parameters in scalar <code>SET</code>
assignments are treated as stored values. More complex statements, including subqueries,
and prepared statements conservatively treat all parameters as stored values.
Column-based INSERT sources require a single row, unquoted identifiers, matching column,
value and argument counts, and only <code>?</code> placeholders or a permutation of
<code>$1</code> through <code>$n</code>. Mixed literals, mixed placeholder styles, dynamic SQL,
and additional SQL clauses are not inferred as password sources.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Hash passwords with a dedicated, salted, slow password hashing function such as bcrypt
(<code>golang.org/x/crypto/bcrypt</code>), scrypt or Argon2 before storing them, and verify login
attempts with the corresponding comparison function rather than direct string equality.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following example stores the password supplied at registration directly in MongoDB:
</p>
<sample language="go">
doc := bson.M{"username": user.Username, "password": user.Password}
return collection.Insert(doc) // BAD
</sample>
<p>
Instead, store a bcrypt hash of the password:
</p>
<sample language="go">
hashed, err := bcrypt.GenerateFromPassword([]byte(user.Password), bcrypt.DefaultCost)
if err != nil {
	return err
}
doc := bson.M{"username": user.Username, "password": string(hashed)}
return collection.Insert(doc) // GOOD
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/256.html">CWE-256</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/312.html">CWE-312</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/522.html">CWE-522</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
